        "title": "Graphic Design - Landscape and Taxonomy",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Graphic Design — Visual Reduction.**\nThe grid is a constraint machine. Typography establishes hierarchy by making\nmost text subordinate so a few elements dominate. White space is not absence;\nit is the structural force that separates signal from noise. Muller-Brockmann's\nSwiss grid, Rand's single-mark logos, Vignelli's restrained palette — every\ncanonical move in the field is a subtraction. The designer's job is not to fill\nthe canvas. It is to empty it until only the message remains."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#009",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:graphic-design:00-overview, git-history:graphic-design:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "graphic-design/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "graphic-design/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:graphic-design:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "graphic-design",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "graphic-design",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Graphic Design - Landscape and Taxonomy",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Comics & Sequential Art — Narrative Reduction.**\nMcCloud's closure theory is the formal account: the reader's imagination does\nmore work than the artist's pen. The gutter between panels is where time,\nmotion, and causation happen — none of it drawn. A splash page earns its impact\nby the panels that *were not* splash pages. Manga's aspect-to-aspect transitions\nlinger in a scene without advancing plot — editing the narrative to exclude\nforward motion, forcing the reader to inhabit a moment. The best sequential\nartists know that what they choose not to draw is the story."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#010",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:graphic-design:00-overview, git-history:graphic-design:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "graphic-design/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "graphic-design/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:graphic-design:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "graphic-design",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "graphic-design",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Graphic Design - Landscape and Taxonomy",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Fashion — Collection Editing.**\nA runway show is 30-40 looks. A designer may sketch 300. The creative director's\nrole is editorial: cut until the collection has a thesis. Silhouette, proportion,\nfabric — these are the signal. Everything else is noise. Haute couture exists as\nthe extreme case: each piece is so reduced in production volume (dozens per season)\nthat every stitch must earn its place. Fast fashion inverts the principle — it\nadds everything, edits nothing, and drowns in volume. The quality gap between\ntiers is not craftsmanship alone; it is editorial discipline."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#011",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:graphic-design:00-overview, git-history:graphic-design:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "graphic-design/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "graphic-design/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:graphic-design:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "graphic-design",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "graphic-design",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Graphic Design - Landscape and Taxonomy",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Sports Science — Performance Editing.**\nAn elite sprinter eliminates wasted lateral motion until every joule transfers\nto forward velocity. A cyclist's power curve is a signal extraction problem: strip\naway junk miles, accumulate load at specific thresholds, taper before competition.\nPeriodization is temporal editing — cut training phases that don't serve the peak.\nThe acute-to-chronic workload ratio is a noise filter: separate the injury-risk\nsignal from the background of daily load variation. The body is the medium; the\nedit is in what training you *don't* do."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#012",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
